MOOP >
MCQ 2.023
An 18-year-old woman has been brought to the Emergency Department for
assessment from the international airport, where she had felt unwell while waiting in
the transit queue to check in for her return flight to an Asian country. She had been
in the country for three weeks only, sightseeing and visiting relatives, and had had
an enjoyable holiday. She said that she was quite anxious about flying and was not
looking forward to returning home. No other symptoms were elicited.
During her appointment she says that she has the strong feeling that she has been
in this Emergency Department before and that everything and everyone there
seems familiar to her. There is no record of her having been to the hospital before
and it would seem that she was only ‘passing through’ the airport for the first time
this morning. Which one of the following is the most likely explanation for her déj@ vu
experience?','{"A":"Depersonalisation.","B":"Capgras syndrome in a patient with schizophrenia.","C":"Temporal lobe epilepsy aura.","D":"Anormal mental state phenomenon.","E":"Head injury.\n\nMCQ 2.024\n\nThe photograph is part of a computed\ntomograph (CT) series of the abdomen\nwith oral contrast administered. The\npatient is a 63-year-old man who had\nan episode of acute epigastric pain two\nmonths before the X-rays were taken.\nHe has presented with persisting upper\nabdominal pain. Which one of the\nfollowing would best explain the current\nradiological findings?\n\nHydatid cyst.\n\nGastrointestinal stromal tumour.\n\n. Splenic cyst.\n\nPeptic ulcer gastric outlet obstruction.\nPeripancreatic pseudocyst.\n\nMOQ2>\n\nSection 2 = Tal Examination |"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","supporting_pages":[],"source_qid":"2.023","duplicate_group_id":"exact-5cf0b936bb85","duplicate_sources":[{"record_id":"d186a8615b8c88bf6a50","record_type":"text_mcq","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_page":69,"source_qid":"2.023"}],"parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-only-conservative-staging-2026-09-22","parser_mode":"strict_ocr_tolerant","text_method":"tesseract","visual_asset":null,"asset_preview_url":null}',NULL,NULL,NULL,NULL,'needs_verification','[]','medium','{"record_id":"d186a8615b8c88bf6a50","source_file":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_relative_path":"96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf","source_year":null,"source_page":69,"source_qid":"2.023","domain_hint":"Mixed/Unclassified","stem":"Australian Medical Council _\nEssential hypertension.\nDiuretic abuse.\n. Renal artery stenosis.\n. Conn syndrome.\nPhaeochromocytoma.\nMOOP >\nMCQ 2.023\nAn 18-year-old woman has been brought to the Emergency Department for\nassessment from the international airport, where she had felt unwell while waiting in\nthe transit queue to check in for her return flight to an Asian country. She had been\nin the country for three weeks only, sightseeing and visiting relatives, and had had\nan enjoyable holiday. She said that she was quite anxious about flying and was not\nlooking forward to returning home. No other symptoms were elicited.\nDuring her appointment she says that she has the strong feeling that she has been\nin this Emergency Department before and that everything and everyone there\nseems familiar to her. There is no record of her having been to the hospital before\nand it would seem that she was only ‘passing through’ the airport for the first time\nthis morning. Which one of the following is the most likely explanation for her déj@ vu\nexperience?","options":{"A":"Depersonalisation.","B":"Capgras syndrome in a patient with schizophrenia.","C":"Temporal lobe epilepsy aura.","D":"Anormal mental state phenomenon.","E":"Head injury.\n\nMCQ 2.024\n\nThe photograph is part of a computed\ntomograph (CT) series of the abdomen\nwith oral contrast administered. The\npatient is a 63-year-old man who had\nan episode of acute epigastric pain two\nmonths before the X-rays were taken.\nHe has presented with persisting upper\nabdominal pain. Which one of the\nfollowing would best explain the current\nradiological findings?\n\nHydatid cyst.\n\nGastrointestinal stromal tumour.\n\n. Splenic cyst.\n\nPeptic ulcer gastric outlet obstruction.\nPeripancreatic pseudocyst.\n\nMOQ2>\n\nSection 2 = Tal Examination |"},"source_answer":null,"source_explanation":null,"source_answer_status":"pending_import","current_answer":null,"current_answer_status":"needs_verification","current_evidence":[],"currentness_risk":"medium","currentness_keywords":["hypertension"],"likely_visual_question":true,"parser_confidence":"high","parser_mode":"strict_ocr_tolerant","text_method":"tesseract","parser_version":"2.7.1-parser-onl