INSERT INTO canonical_records (record_id,source_file,source_page,domain,stem,options_json,source_answer,source_explanation,provenance_json,visual_asset,visual_url,options_mode,current_answer,current_answer_status,current_evidence_json,currentness_risk,record_json,active_quiz,import_version,imported_at) VALUES ('0101a8b9fda9288822d6','96.Handbook of Multiple Choice Questions (Vernon Marshall).pdf','77','Mixed/Unclassified','Australian Medical Council
Section 2 = Trial Examination
SSS
MCQ 2.047
A 16-year-old girl, whose pubertal breast changes commenced about four years
ago, has had episodes of lower abdominal pain lasting about three days each
month for the last 12 months. She has not yet had her first period. Abdominal
examination, when she is not in pain, shows no evidence of any suprapubic mass
or tenderness. Blood tests have shown she is ovulating. Which one of the following
developmental abnormalities is most likely to be the cause of her abdominal pain?','{"A":"An imperforate hymen.","B":"An absent vagina but normal uterus.","C":"A transverse vaginal septum.","D":"Mullerian (paramesonephric) agenesis.","E":"A uterus obstructed at the level of the cervix.\n\neee SS ESSE\n\nMCQ 2.048\n\nA 35-year-old woman presents with cyclical mastalgia. Clinical examination of\nthe breasts is normal. Her mother developed breast cancer at the age of 45 years\nand subsequently died from metastases. The patient''s maternal grandmother also\nhad breast cancer before the age of 50. The patient is on the oral contraceptive\npill (OCP) and no other medications; her general health is normal. Mammography\nis normal. An ultrasound of the breasts shows an uncomplicated cyst with no\nconcerning features in the right breast. Apart from advice about use of simple\nanalgesics and evening primrose oil for her mastalgia, which one of the following is\nthe most appropriate advice in the patient''s followup regimen?\n\nA. Cease the OCP, yearly clinical review, yearly mammography and ultrasound.\n\nB. Remain on the OCP, yearly clinical review, yearly mammography and\nultrasound.\n\nC. Remain on the OCP, six-monthly clinical review, yearly mammography and\nultrasound.\n\nD. Remain on the OCP, two-yearly clinical and mammography review.\n\nE. Cease the OCP, yearly clinical and ultrasound review.\n\nMCQ 2.049\n\nA 31-year-old man is treated with triple therapy (a proton pump inhibitor and two\nantibiotics) for an endoscopically proven duodenal ulcer. He has tested positive for\nHelicobacter pylori. After 10 days of treatment he has improved but is not yet fully\nsymptom-free. Which one of the following is the most appropriate followup for the\npatient?\n\nSerum gastrin estimation.\n\nUrea breath test at six weeks.\n\n. Repeat endoscopy at four weeks.\n\nSerology for H. pylori antibodies at two months.\n\nRepeat endoscopy at two months.\n\nMOQS>"}',NULL,NULL,'{"source_relat